course: biology
lesson: 201
title: Guinea pigs: wheeks, popcorning and vitamin C
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A guinea pig is neither a pig nor from Guinea: it is a rodent from the mountains of South America, a plump cousin of mice and rats with no tail you can see.
explain: Open the fridge in a house with guinea pigs and you will hear a loud whistle, «wheek wheek!», because they have learned that the fridge door means peppers and lettuce.
explain: A happy young guinea pig sometimes leaps straight up and twists in the air like popcorn in a pan, which owners call popcorning.
explain: Guinea pigs are prey animals, so they love a little house to hide in, friends of their own kind, hay all day and fresh vegetables full of vitamin C, which their bodies cannot make.
fact: A guinea pig is a rodent from South America, not a pig and not from Guinea; it has four toes on each front foot, three on each back foot and no visible tail.
fact: Guinea pigs learn sounds linked to food, such as a fridge door or a rustling bag, and call for it with a loud whistle called wheeking.
fact: Popcorning is when a guinea pig jumps straight up and twists in the air; young ones do it when happy and excited, and older ones do it less.
fact: Like people, guinea pigs cannot make their own vitamin C, so they need it every day from foods such as peppers and leafy greens, or they get scurvy with sore joints and bleeding gums.
fact: Hay is a guinea pig's main food: it gives fibre and wears down teeth that never stop growing; guinea pigs also eat some of their own soft droppings, which is normal.
fact: Guinea pigs live in groups and get lonely alone, so they should be kept at least in pairs, and in Switzerland this is even the law.
fact: Guinea pigs talk with wheeks, purrs, rumbles and chutters; a deep rumble while strutting shows one is trying to be in charge, while a soft purr during stroking usually means it is content.
fact: A guinea pig is shy because it is a prey animal; it hides when frightened, so it is best to sit on the floor and let it come to you.
fact: Guinea pig mothers are pregnant for about two months, a long time for a rodent, so the babies are born with fur, open eyes and teeth, run within about an hour and nibble solid food within days.
fact: Guinea pigs come from mild mountain areas and do not like cold, damp or great heat, so many owners bring them indoors in winter.
fact: Guinea pigs often nap with their eyes open, only for a few minutes at a time, ready to run.
fact: People in the Andes tamed guinea pigs thousands of years ago and kept them as farm animals; they reached Europe on ships about five hundred years ago.
exercise: What kind of animal is a guinea pig? | A rodent from South America | A small pig from Africa | A baby rabbit from Guinea | A kind of hairy hamster from Asia
exercise: Why does a guinea pig whistle loudly when the fridge opens? | It has learned the sound means food is coming | It is scared of the cold air coming out of the fridge | It is calling a guinea pig in the fridge | It whistles at any noise without reason
exercise: What is popcorning? | A happy jump straight up with a twist in the air | A guinea pig eating popcorn | A sign of a bad tummy ache after eating too much | A guinea pig hiccupping when it is very cold
exercise: Which guinea pigs popcorn most? | Young ones that are happy and excited | Old ones that are tired | Only guinea pigs that are ill | Only guinea pigs living alone
exercise: Why do guinea pigs need peppers and leafy greens every day? | They cannot make their own vitamin C | They cannot drink water | Greens make their fur curly and soft | Vegetables stop them from sleeping too much in the day
exercise: What illness does a guinea pig get without vitamin C? | Scurvy, with sore joints and bleeding gums | A cold that makes it sneeze all through the winter | Chickenpox with red spots | Hay fever from too much hay
exercise: What should most of a guinea pig's food be? | Hay | Sunflower seeds | Cheese | Bread
exercise: Why must a guinea pig chew hay all day? | Its teeth never stop growing and hay wears them down | Hay keeps its fur warm | Hay is the only thing it can smell, so it needs it to find its way | It needs hay to sleep standing up
exercise: Why do families keep two guinea pigs instead of one? | Guinea pigs live in groups and get lonely alone | Two guinea pigs eat less than one, which saves money | One guinea pig always fights its owner if it is on its own | Guinea pigs can only fall asleep when there are two
exercise: Why does a guinea pig hide in its little house when a child comes? | It is a shy prey animal that feels safe hiding | It is angry with the child and does not want to see it | It is building a warm nest inside for the winter | It wants the child to follow it inside and play
exercise: How do baby guinea pigs look when they are born? | With fur, open eyes and teeth | Bald and blind, like baby mice | Inside eggs that hatch a week later | Without teeth, needing milk for months
exercise: What does a deep rumble from a strutting guinea pig usually mean? | It is trying to be in charge | It is very hungry and wants its dinner | It is about to fall asleep | It is calling its babies
exercise: Why is a garden hutch in winter a problem for a guinea pig? | Cold and damp can make it ill | It will hibernate and never wake | It grows far too much thick fur | Snow is poisonous to guinea pigs
exercise: How does a guinea pig often sleep? | In short naps, sometimes with its eyes open | For twelve hours with its eyes shut, like a cat | Hanging upside down | Only in winter, for months
exercise: Where were guinea pigs first tamed? | In the Andes in South America | In Guinea in Africa, as the name says | In China | In the forests of Europe
exercise: How did guinea pigs reach Europe? | On ships about five hundred years ago | By swimming across the ocean | They always lived in Europe | On planes about fifty years ago
exercise: How many toes does a guinea pig have on each back foot? | Three | Four | Five | Two
test: «my bro says guinea pigs r just baby pigs lol» What is right? | No, they are rodents from South America | Yes, they grow into big farm pigs in a year | Yes, they are pigs from Guinea | No, they are baby rabbits
test: In a cartoon a guinea pig swings its long fluffy tail. What is wrong with the cartoon? | Real guinea pigs have no tail you can see | Real guinea pigs have two short tails | Real guinea pigs have no legs | Nothing, their tails really are very long and fluffy
test: «2 guinea pigs, count their toes, all the feet» How many toes in total? | 28 | 32 | 40 | 24
test: What would happen if a guinea pig got only dry pellets and no vitamin C for weeks? | It could get scurvy, with sore joints and bleeding gums | It would grow faster | Nothing at all, because it makes its own vitamin C inside its body | It would start to hibernate
test: «Bought one guinea pig, she's fine alone innit» What is the best answer? | No, guinea pigs are social and need a friend of their own kind | Yes, they hate other guinea pigs and fight them all day long | Yes, a mirror is all she needs | No, she needs a cat for company
test: «shut up Max, your guinea pig's broken, it keeps jumping up and twisting» What is right? | Nothing is broken: it is popcorning because it is happy | It is having a fit and must go to sleep in a dark room at once | It is trying to fly away | It has fleas biting its feet
test: A guinea pig stares with open eyes and does not move for a few minutes. What may be happening? | It may be napping with its eyes open | It must be dead | It is counting the child | It is pretending to be a rock forever
test: A child grabs at a guinea pig and it runs into its house. What should the child do? | Sit quietly on the floor and let it come out | Pull it out of the house quickly | Shout so it gets used to noise | Take the house away so it cannot hide and learns to be brave
test: «Teeth keep growin? so why don't they get massive» What is right? | Chewing hay all day wears them down | They fall out every month | Guinea pigs file them with stones | They only grow in summer
test: A guinea pig mother has babies on Monday. What can they do by Monday evening? | Run about with open eyes | Nothing, they are blind for weeks | Flap about and try to fly | Only drink milk lying still
test: A guinea pig is seen eating its own soft droppings. What should the owner think? | This is normal and helps it get more goodness from food | It is very ill and needs surgery | It is bored and must get a toy | It is starving and needs meat and fish added to its dinner every day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the guinea pig? | It is a rodent of South American origin, domesticated in the Andes | It is a juvenile pig of African origin | It is a marsupial native to Australia | It is a hybrid of the rabbit and the hamster
exam: Identify the principal reason for which fresh vegetables must be furnished to a guinea pig daily. | The animal is incapable of producing vitamin C within its own body | The animal is incapable of digesting hay | The animal requires vegetables in order to sleep | The animal derives its water solely from cabbage
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the keeping of guinea pigs. | They are social animals and ought to be kept in the company of at least one other | They are solitary and attack all companions placed within their enclosure | They thrive in cold and damp garden hutches | They require no hiding place whatsoever
exam: It is given that a young guinea pig leaps vertically and twists in mid-air. Ascertain the most probable explanation. | The behaviour, termed popcorning, signifies excitement and contentment | The behaviour signifies an acute deficiency of vitamin C | The behaviour signifies an attempt to escape a predator | The behaviour signifies the onset of hibernation
